#
# TODO - cmd
#
# Untracked working notes: what /bin still carries without a source.
#
# /bin has 118 entries and exactly two build signatures.  Fifty-nine
# begin "99 97 ... d1 21" and are ccc's, built and installed by this
# tree.  Fifty-nine begin "99 94 ... c1 21", or carry no 16 byte a.out
# header at all and start straight into a c1 21 prologue, and are
# Whitesmiths' - the binaries the distribution floppies seeded.  The
# split is exact, 59 and 59, with nothing left over, so the question is
# not what is in /bin but which of the 59 the distribution left we have
# a source for.
#
# Of those 59, in order of how little is missing:
#
#	3  the source is here and the build never installs it -
#	   mkfs, fsck, far
#	1  cpp - /bin/cpp has no counterpart in this tree at all.  See
#	   below.
#	2  the source is a part of a bigger program -
#	   edit (cmd/vi), help (cmd/less)
#	8  the Whitesmith toolchain: as cc link lib obj hex lord anat.
#	   attic/ has sources for all of them, and it does not matter -
#	   see below.
#	17 the source is in extra/, v6 and v7, unported -
#	   cal comm cu file login lpr mail passwd ps su sum tail tty
#	   update wall who
#	28 nothing in the repository.
#
# THE WHITESMITH TOOLCHAIN IS NOT A WORK ITEM
#
# as cc link lib obj hex lord anat are the compiler chain the
# distribution was built with, and every one of them is superseded by
# something this tree builds: as by asz, cc by ccc, link by ld, lib by
# ar, obj by nm.  Regenerating them from attic/ is not wanted, so their
# /bin copies are history rather than a gap - there is nothing to
# rebuild them for, and no reason to carry their sources forward.
# Where /bin has one and we have a replacement, the fix is to drop the
# old name or let it age out, not to find it a build.
#
# /BIN/CPP IS NOT pass0
#
# ccc runs five programs out of /libexec, and not one of them is a
# preprocessor:
#
#	pass0	the lexer and normalizer; its source directory is
#		libexec/pass0
#	c0	pass1, the parser
#	c1	pass2, the code generator
#	peep	the peephole, under -O
#	astpp	AST pretty printer - a debugging aid, not in the
#		compile path at all
#
# pass0 emits a .x stream, "compact token format", and that stream is
# its output: it is what c0 reads, not text anything else consumes.  It
# has text modes, -p writing a readable .i beside the .x and -E handing
# the .x to xdump to render as text, but both are debugging aids over
# the lexeme stream, not a preprocessor's macro-expanded source.
#
# The name cpp is inherited from where the code came from and is wrong.
# The program is pass0, /libexec holds it as pass0, ccc's driver builds
# the path with "pass0" in the format string, and the source directory
# is libexec/pass0 now.  What still says cpp is inside the sources
# themselves - cpp.c and cpp.h - and the driver's own variable and
# header comment ("Orchestrates cpp, c0 (parser), and c1 (code
# generator)").  Renaming those is cosmetic and has not been done.
#
# So /bin/cpp is not the same program as anything this tree builds: it
# is a standalone preprocessor of the ordinary filter kind, and it is a
# gap rather than a duplicate whose install path wants fixing.
#
# If a standalone cpp is wanted, the only source for one here is
# attic/hitech/cpp - John Reiser's cpp, the 1978 one, as maintained by
# Schilling and Ogden, not Whitesmith's.  It targets CP/M and would
# need the usual porting.
#
# Two more in the 28 are the same kind of thing and are marked in place
# below: cxr, ptc and rp are Whitesmith's own tools (a C
# cross-referencer, the Pascal compiler, a macro processor) and ddt is
# a debugger.  None of them is a utility anyone is missing.
#
# THE 28 WITH NO SOURCE ANYWHERE IN THE REPOSITORY
#
# What each is, read off the binary where the binary says - a usage
# string, a keyword table.  Six say nothing at all and are marked so
# rather than guessed at: clean, dc, ddt, down, pilot, unique.
#
#	chars	count characters; with lines and words, the three text
#		counters, all about 5.5k and near-identical
#	clean	?
#	cp1	the first half of the overlay-based cp
#	cp2	the second half.  We build cp, so the pair is obsolete.
#	cptree	recursive copy: "Cptree is used to copy an entire tree
#		of files and ..."
#	crypt	crypt(1): "usage: crypt key"
#	cxr	Whitesmith: C cross-referencer, carries the C type keywords
#		VOID TINY ULONG UCOUNT TEXT LOCAL
#	dc	?
#	ddt	? - a debugger, the DDT name
#	down	? - 384 bytes, the smallest thing in /bin
#	group	chgrp by another name: "usage: group [-newgroup] file ..."
#	lines	count lines
#	lprm	printer queue, remove entries
#	lprq	printer queue, query
#	newuser	add a user
#	owner	chown: "usage: owner [-newowner] file ..."
#	pilot	?
#	print	"usage: print [-l#] [-w#] [-#] [file ...]"
#	ptc	Whitesmith: the Pascal compiler - "goto target", "file in
#		value argument" (disks/1070-8_pascal.image, disks/whitesmith)
#	rp	Whitesmith: a macro processor of the include-file kind -
#		"can't open include file", "arg stack overflow"
#	sort	"usage: sort [-bdfrtx] [+key] [-o output] input ..."
#	stty	terminal modes
#	td	tape dump: "Dump destination must be either block special
#		or directory."
#	unique	?
#	words	count words
#	write	write(1) - to another user's terminal
#
# WHICH OF THEM MATTER
#
# A full /bin is not the goal.  What has to exist for the system to
# come up and rebuild itself is a shell, the toolchain, make, and the
# file utilities those need - and all of that is in the 63 this tree
# builds, so none of the 26 blocks a bootstrap.  The nearest to it is
# stty, which the terminal needs before anything interactive is
# pleasant.  The rest is a userland that can wait.
#
# The same test is worth applying to the cross DIRS list in
# GNUmakefile: a name belongs there when it is needed to bootstrap the
# others, not merely because a source exists for it.  Everything on
# that list has to be buildable before the thing that builds it.
#
# WHAT THAT MAKES ACTIONABLE
#
# mkfs and fsck pass the bootstrap test and fail the wiring one.  Their
# sources are here, their makefiles are maintained, and the guest
# builds them - filesystem/usr/src/cmd/mkfs/mkfs is 12187 bytes and
# ccc's 99 97, the /bin one is 6894 and Whitesmiths' 99 94.  Neither
# name is in the guest cmd/makefile DIRS or in the cross GNUmakefile
# DIRS, so the built copies are never installed and /bin keeps running
# the 1980s ones.  mkfs is what makes the filesystem we install onto,
# which makes it the first thing that list should carry.
#
# halt and upm are the mirror image: in the cross DIRS, not in the
# guest's, so /bin has our ccc-built copies that the guest could not
# rebuild for itself.
#
# vim: tabstop=4 shiftwidth=4 noexpandtab:
